  "Number": 20,
  "Question": "When \\(10^{93} - 93\\) is expressed as a single whole number, the sum of the digits is",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(10\\)",
    "B": "\\(93\\)",
    "C": "\\(819\\)",
    "D": "\\(826\\)",
    "E": "\\(833\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Subtracting \\(1\\) first makes the long subtraction easier. The number \\(10^{93}\\) is \\(1\\) followed by \\(93\\) zeros, so \\(10^{93}-1\\) consists of \\(93\\) nines.\n\nTo subtract \\(93\\) in all, we must now subtract another \\(92\\):\n\\[\n10^{93}-93=(10^{93}-1)-92.\n\\]\nThe last two digits change from \\(99\\) to \\(07\\), since \\(99-92=7\\). This requires no borrowing from the earlier digits, so the first \\(91\\) digits remain nines. Hence the digit sum is\n\\[\n91\\times9+0+7=826.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(826\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).",
